# Apple platform multi-user / profile isolation — measured evidence

**Method note:** WebSearch not used. All results via direct-URL fetch (support.apple.com, developer docs), NVD REST API 2.0, Crossref, arXiv, HN Algolia. **OpenAlex ran out of daily budget mid-sweep** ("Insufficient budget… Resets at midnight UTC") — partial results only, marked below.

---

## 1. Shared iPad (iPadOS) — the only real multi-user surface Apple ships

**Primary sources (both reached in full):**
- Apple Platform Security → *Shared iPad security in iPadOS*: `https://support.apple.com/guide/security/shared-ipad-security-secd99f373ef/web` (Published Date: **February 18, 2021** — note: the security guide's Shared iPad page has not been revised in 5+ years)
- Apple Platform Deployment → *Shared iPad overview*: `https://support.apple.com/guide/deployment/shared-ipad-overview-dep9a34c2ba2/web` (Published **October 25, 2023**)
- Apple Platform Deployment → *Prepare Shared iPad*: `https://support.apple.com/guide/deployment/prepare-shared-ipad-dep6fa9dd532/web` (Published **December 11, 2023**)

*(The URLs you supplied — `sec110ec5e2b`, `dep9a3a2acd` — are dead; the live IDs are `secd99f373ef` and `dep9a34c2ba2`.)*

### 1a. Data separation model — VENDOR CLAIM (design), no measurement found

> "Shared iPad is a multiuser mode for use in iPad deployments. It allows users to share an iPad while maintaining separation of documents and data for each user. **Each user gets their own private, reserved storage location, which is implemented as an Apple File System (APFS) volume protected by the user's credential.**"

> "**User data is partitioned into separate directories, each in their own data protection domains and protected by both UNIX permissions and sandboxing.**"

So the claimed stack is four layers: APFS volume boundary + per-user Data Protection domain (keybag) + UNIX uid + sandbox.

### 1b. Keychain / key material IS per-user

> "If the user hasn't used the device before or is using the temporary session feature, Shared iPad **provisions a new UNIX user ID, an APFS volume to store the user's personal data, and a local keychain.**"

> "When a user signs out of Shared iPad, **that user's keybag is immediately locked and all apps are shut down.**"

> "After background syncing for that user is complete, **the user's APFS volume is unmounted and can't be mounted again without the user signing back in.**"

**Secure Enclave: NOT REACHED as a per-user statement.** The Shared iPad security page never mentions the Secure Enclave. It describes per-user *keybags* and per-user *local keychains*, but Apple publishes no statement that SEP-resident material (biometric templates, the SEP's own key hierarchy) is partitioned per Shared iPad user. Biometrics on Shared iPad are effectively absent from the doc entirely — sign-in is passcode/federated-credential based:
> "Setup Assistant on Shared iPad prompts the user to establish a passcode (credential) used to secure the local data on the device and to authenticate to the login screen in the future."

### 1c. Managed Apple ID — REQUIRED, no exceptions except Guest

> "**Shared iPad requires the use of a Managed Apple Account that's issued and owned by the organization.**"
> "Shared iPad requires a device management service and Managed Apple Accounts that an organization issues and owns… **Devices need to have at least 32 GB of storage and be supervised.**"

Both native and federated Managed Apple Accounts work; federated goes through the IdP once, then "a short-lived access token is issued for the backing Managed Apple Accounts." Native accounts authenticate to Apple Identity Service (IDS) **using the SRP protocol**.

Supported hardware (a real limit): "All iPad Pro models; iPad (5th generation) or later; iPad Air 2 or later; iPad mini (4th generation) or later."

### 1d. Quota — MEASURED numbers, published by Apple

From *Prepare Shared iPad*:
> "**10 GB for the system, 8 GB for apps and media. The remaining storage is divided among the number of defined users, with 1 GB minimum per user.** Note: Photos won't sync with a Managed Apple Account for quotas of 1 GB."
> "Devices with a storage capacity 64 GB or greater: **10 GB for the system, 16 GB for apps and media. The remaining storage is divided among the number of defined users, with 2 GB minimum per user.**"
> "For example, if you specify a value of five users on a 128 GB iPad, **each user has approximately 20.4 GB of local storage**."

MDM levers (with minimum OS): `Maximum number of users` (iPadOS 13.4), `Set the quota size` — "in megabytes, for each user" (iPadOS 13.4), `Delete user` (iOS 9.3), `Sign out user` (iOS 9.3), `Temporary session only` (iPadOS 14.5), `Sign-in grace period` — days before network auth is required, "Setting this to 0 requires network authentication each time" (iPadOS 16.1).

**Apple publishes no hard cap on user count.** The cap is emergent from quota arithmetic: at 2 GB minimum on a 64 GB device you get ~19 cached users; a 32 GB device at 1 GB min gets ~14. Apple's own guidance is to game this:
> "It's recommended that you set the maximum storage value for users as low as possible to **maximize the number of cached users**, minimize communication with iCloud, and provide a faster sign-in experience."

### 1e. Eviction — the most load-bearing limit, and it fails closed *and* loudly

> "**Because storage is allocated (reserved) for the user at the time the APFS volume is created, there may be insufficient space to create a new volume.** In such an event, the system identifies an existing user **whose data has finished syncing to the cloud** and evicts that user from the device so that the new user to sign in. **In the unlikely event that all existing users haven't completed uploading their cloud data, the new user sign in fails.** To sign in, the new user needs to wait for the previous user's data to finish syncing, or **have an administrator forcibly delete an existing user account, thereby risking data loss.**"

And from the deployment guide: "**If additional space is needed for a new user, the local data for the oldest user is removed.**"

This is the architecturally interesting bit: **eviction is gated on cloud-sync completion, so iCloud is a hard dependency for the multi-user property, not a convenience.** A Shared iPad that can't reach iCloud eventually cannot admit a new user at all.

### 1f. Offline — bounded, and the bound is admin-set

> "If the device isn't connected to the internet… authentication can occur against the local account **for a limited number of days**. In that situation, **only users with previously existing local accounts or a temporary session can sign in.** After the time limit has expired, users are required to authenticate online, even if a local account already exists."

Apple does not publish the default day count in the security guide; the deployment guide exposes it as the MDM `Sign-in grace period` (iPadOS 16.1+). Also: "In iPadOS 16.1 or later, Shared iPad **defaults to using the local passcode for existing users on the device, and so requires no network connection.**"

### 1g. Temporary / Guest sessions — deletion is immediate and total

> "When the user signs out of a temporary session, **their APFS volume is deleted and its reserved space is returned to the system.**"
> "When a temporary session is ended, Shared iPad performs the full logout sequence and **deletes the temporary session's APFS volume immediately.**"
> "**Temporary sessions don't sync data with iCloud**, and although a temporary session can sign into a third-party syncing service such as Box or Google Drive, **there's no facility to continue syncing data when the temporary session ends.**"
> (deployment) "any user has the ability to initiate a temporary session without the need for a user name or password by tapping Guest at the login screen… **In a temporary session, any user can unlock and access the iPad without a password.** Users working in a temporary session should take this into account if they're signed in to any websites or apps."

### 1h. The documented soft spot — a ~30-second deferred-cleanup window

> "To accelerate the case of a new user signing in, **iPadOS defers some ordinary sign-out actions temporarily** and presents a sign-in window to the new user. **If a user signs in during this time (approximately 30 seconds), Shared iPad performs the deferred cleanup as part of signing in to the new user account.**"

Apple states the keybag locks immediately and apps are killed immediately; what is deferred is unspecified "ordinary sign-out actions." **No public research measures what remains live during that window.** This is the one Apple-acknowledged place where the isolation boundary is time-dependent rather than cryptographic.

### 1i. Other stated limits
- "Users **can't delete apps** on Shared iPad."
- "Users **can't save passwords using Safari AutoFill** unless a user configuration profile specifying the allowed domain… is installed."
- "In iPadOS 16.6 or earlier with Shared iPad, user profiles like Mail, Exchange ActiveSync, or CalDAV **get installed only if they don't contain sensitive information such as a passcode or password**, which requires that the profile be encrypted."
- Temporary sessions "prevent users from modifying account settings or signing in to Apple services… users can't sign in to Messages or the App Store."

### 1j. Shared iPad CVE count: **ZERO**

NVD keyword `Shared iPad` → 7 results, **none about Shared iPad** (all false hits on the word "shared": CVE-2020-9777 Mail video, CVE-2020-9868 cert validation, CVE-2019-8857 Live Photo iCloud links, CVE-2023-23526 iCloud shared-by-me Gatekeeper, CVE-2022-46710 location in iCloud links, CVE-2025-43200, CVE-2025-43510).
`https://services.nvd.nist.gov/rest/json/cves/2.0?keywordSearch=Shared%20iPad`

**FINDING (negative): in the entire NVD corpus there is not one CVE describing a cross-user data leak on Shared iPad.** Two readings, and the evidence does not separate them: either the design holds, or nobody with disclosure discipline is looking at an education-fleet feature. Given zero academic papers either (§4), the second reading deserves weight. Absence of CVEs here is **absence of evidence, not evidence of absence.**

---

## 2. iOS consumer multi-user: does not exist

### 2a. Confirmation is structural, not a quotable denial

**NOT REACHED as a positive statement.** Apple publishes no sentence saying "iPhone does not support multiple users." The confirmation is by exhaustion of the Apple Platform Security guide's own table of contents (fetched in full from `https://support.apple.com/guide/security/welcome/web`): under *Secure device management* the **only** multiuser entry is "Shared iPad security." There is no iPhone equivalent, no consumer-mode equivalent, no macOS-style account section for iOS. Corroborating positive statement, from the security page itself: "**Shared iPad is a multiuser mode for use in iPad deployments**" — deployments, i.e. MDM + Managed Apple Account + supervision, per §1c.

So: **the multi-user boundary on Apple mobile is gated behind organizational ownership of the identity.** A family cannot reach it. This is a licensing/identity gate, not a technical one — the APFS-volume machinery exists on every iPad in the supported list.

### 2b. What Apple offers instead

**Guided Access** — `https://support.apple.com/en-us/111795` (Published **July 24, 2026**). Single-app lockdown, not isolation:
> "you can temporarily restrict your device to a single app. This can be useful when you let a child use your device, or you want to stay focused on a task."

Escape is by "Triple-click the side button… Authenticate with your Guided Access passcode, Face ID, or Touch ID." Note the Face ID/Touch ID option: **if the child is an enrolled Face ID/Touch ID identity on that iPhone, they can end the session.** Apple also documents a genuine safety cost: "**Important: Crash Detection and Emergency Services aren't available while using Guided Access.**"

**Screen Time** — Platform Security page `https://support.apple.com/guide/security/screen-time-security-secd8831e732/web` (Published **February 18, 2021**). Apple's own framing is explicit that this is *not* a security boundary:
> "**Although Screen Time isn't a new system security feature**, it's important to understand how it protects the privacy and security of the data gathered and shared between devices."

> "There are two types of users: adults and (managed) children."
> "When parents turn on Screen Time for their children, the parents set a passcode so their children can't make changes. **When they reach age of majority (age will vary depending on country or region), the children can turn this monitoring off.**"

Transport is E2E: usage syncs "using CloudKit end-to-end encryption… requires that the user's account have two-factor authentication enabled"; parent↔child config moves over "the end-to-end encrypted Apple Identity Service (IDS) protocol… **This data isn't readable by Apple.**"

Content & Privacy Restrictions inventory: `https://support.apple.com/en-us/105121` (Published **April 21, 2026**).

**Locked and Hidden apps (iOS 18)** — `https://support.apple.com/guide/iphone/lock-or-hide-or-an-app-iph00f208d05/ios`. Apple states the limits plainly, and they are substantial:

> "Locking an app requires Face ID, Touch ID, or your passcode to open the app."
> "**Note: Some of the apps that come with iPhone can't be locked—including Calculator, Camera, Clock, Contacts, Find My, Maps, Shortcuts, and Settings.**"
> "Hiding an app prevents others from seeing or opening it (unless they have a way to authenticate your iPhone using Face ID, Touch ID, or a passcode). **When you hide an app, its name is still visible in some places, such as Screen Time, Battery Usage By App (in Settings), and your App Store purchase history.**"
> "**Note: Apps that come installed can't be hidden**—only apps you download separately from the App Store can be hidden. Apps that are set by the user as default web browser or app marketplace (available only in the EU) can't be hidden either."

This is **UI concealment with a re-auth prompt, not a data boundary**, and Apple documents three separate side channels (Screen Time, Battery Usage By App, App Store purchase history) that leak the app's name by design. Anyone who can unlock the phone can unhide.

### 2c. CVEs on bypassing these: essentially none

| Query | Scope | Total | Apple-relevant |
|---|---|---|---|
| `Guided Access` | all NVD | 91 | **0** — every hit is Oracle Commerce Guided Search, PeopleSoft "Guided Self Service", etc. Zero Apple. |
| `Screen Time` | all NVD | 43 | 1 (below) |
| `Screen Time` | + `cpe:2.3:o:apple:iphone_os` | 3 | 1 real |
| `Screen Time restrictions` | all NVD | 1 | 0 (Ubuntu indicator-datetime) |
| `hidden apps iOS` | all NVD | **0** | 0 |
| `restrictions bypass iOS parental` | all NVD | 1 | 1 (2012) |

The two that exist:
- **CVE-2020-9775** (pub 2020-04-01, CVSS **5.3 MEDIUM**), fixed **iOS 13.4 / iPadOS 13.4**: *"An issue existed in the handling of tabs displaying picture in picture video… **A user's private browsing activity may be unexpectedly saved in Screen Time.**"* — a Screen Time *leak*, the inverse of a bypass.
- **CVE-2012-3741** (pub 2012-09-20, CVSS **1.9 LOW**), fixed **iOS 6**: *"The Restrictions (aka Parental Controls) implementation in Apple iOS before 6 does not properly handle purchase attempts after a Disable Restrictions action, which allows local users to bypass an intended Apple ID authentication step."*

**FINDING (negative, strong): there is no CVE for bypassing Guided Access, and none for bypassing Screen Time restrictions or Locked/Hidden apps.** Fourteen years, zero. The reason is almost certainly classification, not absence of bypasses: **Apple does not treat parental-control evasion as a security vulnerability**, so it never enters the CVE pipeline. The known bypass corpus lives entirely in practitioner/consumer channels — HN Algolia surfaced only consumer-grade material ("Kids Are Hacking Famous Apple's Parent Control System," 2019-04-25, `https://bytescout.com/blog/bypass-screen-time-on-ipad-or-7-hacks-found-by-kids-to-walkaround-apples-parent-control.html`) and, tellingly, **a cluster of 2026 Show HN products whose entire premise is that Screen Time is trivially bypassable** — WaitToUnlock (`https://waittounlock.com/`, posted 2026-07-10, 2026-07-13, 2026-07-26, 2026-08-22, "makes your Screen Time limits unbypassable"), Nopin (2025-12-27, "I made iOS Screen Time limits harder to bypass"). A third-party market exists to patch it. That is market-revealed evidence of weakness, though **not a measured bypass**.

**NOT REACHED:** I could not reach a rigorous published write-up of the specific Screen Time passcode weaknesses (the old iOS-11-and-earlier Restrictions-passcode-hash-in-backup recovery, or the 2024–25 Screen Time web-filter bypass reports). The Mysk blog index at `https://www.mysk.blog/` returned only `<h1>Mysk Blog</h1>` — a JS-rendered SPA that my fetch could not enumerate. **Do not fill these in from memory; they are unverified here.**

