## 3. macOS fast user switching / multi-account isolation — MEASURED, and it recurs

This is where the evidence is real. NVD queries:
`https://services.nvd.nist.gov/rest/json/cves/2.0?keywordSearch=fast%20user%20switching` (11 total) and `?keywordSearch=another+user&virtualMatchString=cpe:2.3:o:apple:macos` (10 total).

### 3a. The fast-user-switching desktop-leak, three times in three years

| CVE | Published | CVSS | Fixed in | Apple's wording |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **CVE-2021-30908** | 2021-08-24 | 6.5 | macOS Monterey 12.0.1, Big Sur 11.6.1 | "An authentication issue was addressed with improved state management… **A local attacker may be able to view the previous logged-in user's desktop from the fast user switching screen.**" |
| **CVE-2022-22656** | 2022-03-18 | — | macOS Big Sur 11.6.5, Monterey 12.3, Security Update 2022-003 Catalina | "An authentication issue was addressed with improved state management… **A local attacker may be able to view the previous logged in user's desktop from the fast user switching screen.**" |
| **CVE-2023-42935** | 2024-01-23 | — | macOS Ventura 13.6.4 | "An authentication issue was addressed with improved state management… **A local attacker may be able to view the previous logged in user's desktop from the fast user switching screen.**" |

**These three descriptions are byte-for-byte the same bug, fixed three times.** Verified in Apple's own release notes — `https://support.apple.com/en-us/120307` (macOS Ventura 13.6.4, Published June 3, 2024, "Entry updated April 24, 2024"):

> "**LoginWindow** / Available for: macOS Ventura / Impact: A local attacker may be able to view the previous logged in user's desktop from the fast user switching screen / Description: An authentication issue was addressed with improved state management. **CVE-2023-42935: ASentientBot**"

The component is named: **LoginWindow**. Same class, same fix language, 2021 → 2022 → 2024. The remediation "improved state management" appearing three consecutive times is the signal: **the FUS session-transition state machine is the defect locus, and each fix patched an instance rather than the machine.** If you want one measured fact about Apple multi-account isolation, this is it.

### 3b. Adjacent cross-user failures, same era

| CVE | Published | CVSS | Fixed in | Impact |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **CVE-2023-42861** | 2023-10-25 | 6.5 | macOS Sonoma 14.1 | "**An attacker with knowledge of a standard user's credentials can unlock another standard user's locked screen on the same Mac.**" (logic issue, improved state management) |
| **CVE-2023-32654** | 2023-07-28 | 6.5 | macOS Ventura 13.5 | "A logic issue was addressed with improved state management. **A user may be able to read information belonging to another user.**" |
| **CVE-2024-23261** | 2024-07-29 | **7.5 HIGH** | macOS Monterey 12.7.6, Sonoma 14.4, Ventura 13.6.8 | "A logic issue was addressed with improved state management. **An attacker may be able to read information belonging to another user.**" |
| **CVE-2020-27893** | 2021-04-02 | 6.5 | macOS Big Sur 11.0.1 | "An issue existed in screen sharing… **A user with screen sharing access may be able to view another user's screen.**" |
| **CVE-2024-44248** | 2024-12-12 | 6.5 | macOS Sequoia 15.1, Sonoma 14.7.2, Ventura 13.7.2 | "**A user with screen sharing access may be able to view another user's screen.**" (improved state management) |
| **CVE-2025-43516** | 2025-12-12 | 3.3 | macOS Sequoia 15.7.3, Sonoma 14.8.3, Tahoe 26.2 | "A session management issue… **A user with Voice Control enabled may be able to transcribe another user's activity.**" |
| **CVE-2023-42836** | 2024-02-21 | 5.3 | iOS/iPadOS 17.1, macOS Ventura 13.6.3, Sonoma 14.1, Monterey 12.7.2 | "**An attacker may be able to access connected network volumes mounted in the home directory.**" |

Note CVE-2020-27893 and CVE-2024-44248 are **also a verbatim repeat, four years apart** — screen sharing viewing another user's screen. Two independent recurrence pairs in the same subsystem family.

### 3c. Keychain (`?keywordSearch=keychain&virtualMatchString=cpe:2.3:o:apple:macos`, 9 total)

| CVE | Published | CVSS | Fixed in | Impact |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| CVE-2021-30912 | 2021-08-24 | 5.5 | macOS Monterey 12.0.1, Big Sur 11.6.1, SU 2021-007 Catalina | "**A malicious application may gain access to a user's Keychain items.**" (improved permissions logic) |
| CVE-2024-27837 | 2024-05-14 | 3.3 | macOS Sonoma 14.5 | "A downgrade issue was addressed with additional code-signing restrictions. **A local attacker may gain access to Keychain items.**" |
| CVE-2024-54490 | 2024-12-12 | 5.5 | macOS Sequoia 15.2 | "addressed by enabling hardened runtime. **A local attacker may gain access to user's Keychain items.**" |
| CVE-2025-43251 | 2025-07-30 | 5.5 | macOS Sequoia 15.6 | "An authorization issue was addressed with improved state management. **A local attacker may gain access to Keychain items.**" |
| CVE-2025-31213 | 2025-05-12 | **7.6 HIGH** | iPadOS 17.7.7, macOS Sequoia 15.5, Sonoma 14.7.6, Ventura 13.7.6 | "A logging issue was addressed with improved data redaction. **An app may be able to access associated usernames and websites in a user's iCloud Keychain.**" |
| CVE-2026-28864 | 2026-03-25 | 3.3 | iOS/iPadOS 18.7.7 & 26.4, macOS Sequoia 15.7.5, Sonoma 14.8.5, Tahoe 26.4, visionOS/watchOS 26.4 | "improved permissions checking. **A local attacker may gain access to user's Keychain items.**" |
| CVE-2026-28860 | 2026-05-11 | **7.5 HIGH** | same fleet as above + tvOS 26.4 | "improved input validation. **A local attacker may be able to modify the state of the Keychain.**" |
| CVE-2026-43728 | 2026-07-27 | **7.5 HIGH** | macOS Tahoe 26.6 | "improved state management. **An attacker may be able to modify the state of the Keychain.**" |

**Cadence: at least one keychain-access-by-local-attacker CVE per year, 2021–2026, unbroken.** Note CVE-2026-28864 and CVE-2026-28860 land on **iOS/iPadOS too** — the keychain boundary is not a macOS-only concern, it just has more attack surface there.

### 3d. TCC (`?keywordSearch=TCC&virtualMatchString=cpe:2.3:o:apple:macos`, 6 total)

Only **one is an Apple defect** — CVE-2021-1873 (2021-09-08, 6.5, macOS Big Sur 11.3 / SU 2021-002 Catalina / SU 2021-003 Mojave): "An API issue in Accessibility TCC permissions was addressed with improved state management. **A malicious application may be able to unexpectedly leak a user's credentials from secure text fields.**"

The other five are **third-party apps inheriting Apple's TCC grants** — and they form a clean pattern worth naming:
- CVE-2023-40299 (7.8) Kong Insomnia — `DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES` to "make requests for TCC permissions"
- CVE-2025-8672 (7.8) **GIMP**: "bundles a Python interpreter that **inherits the Transparency, Consent, and Control (TCC) permissions granted by the user to the main application bundle**. An attacker with local user access can invoke this interpreter with arbitrary commands… leveraging the application's previously granted TCC permi[ssions]"
- CVE-2025-14714 (6.5) — identical bundled-interpreter TCC inheritance
- CVE-2025-12843 (5.5) waveterm — "Code Injection using Electron Fuses… allows TCC Bypass"
- CVE-2025-64723 (4.4) Arduino IDE — "overly permissive security entitlements that could bypass macOS Hardened Runtime protections"

**The structural finding: TCC grants attach to a bundle, not to a principal.** Any interpreter inside a granted bundle inherits the grant. That is a design property of TCC, not a bug in any one app — which is why it produces a stream of separately-numbered CVEs against unrelated vendors. Directly relevant to any capability model: **a permission bound to a container is a permission held by everything the container can execute.**

### 3e. Cross-user file reads (`?keywordSearch=read+arbitrary+files&virtualMatchString=cpe:2.3:o:apple:macos`, 30 total; Apple subset)

CVE-2021-1797 (5.5, macOS Big Sur 11.2 + Catalina/Mojave SUs + watchOS 7.3, tvOS 14.4, **iOS/iPadOS 14.4** — "A local user may be able to read arbitrary files"); CVE-2021-30828 (5.5, SU 2021-005 Catalina, Big Sur 11.6 — "as root"); CVE-2022-32838 (5.5, Monterey 12.5, Big Sur 11.6.8, Catalina SU 2022-005, **iOS/iPadOS 15.6**); CVE-2023-23520 (5.9, race condition, Ventura 13.2, **iOS/iPadOS 16.3**, watchOS 9.3, tvOS 16.3 — "as root"); CVE-2023-27955 (5.5); CVE-2023-40406 (5.5); CVE-2023-41968 (5.5, symlink validation); CVE-2023-42887 (6.3, "additional sandbox restrictions", Ventura 13.6.4 / Sonoma 14.2 — Ron Masas of BreakPoint.sh); CVE-2024-23236 (5.5); CVE-2024-27827 (5.5); CVE-2024-44190 (5.5); CVE-2024-44195 (**7.5**, Sequoia 15.1).

**Twelve arbitrary-file-read CVEs across 2021–2024, one every ~3 months.** Several ship the same fix simultaneously to macOS *and* iOS/iPadOS, which tells you the defective path is in shared code, not in the macOS account layer.

### 3f. Historical FUS record (the pattern is 20 years old)

CVE-2005-2509 (Mac OS X ≤10.4.2, loginwindow, "allows attackers to log into other accounts if they know the passwords to at least two accounts"); CVE-2005-2742 (SecurityAgent, "Switch User…" button appears "even though the 'Enable fast user switching' setting is disabled"); CVE-2006-4393 (10.4–10.4.7, "allows local users to gain access to **Kerberos tickets of other users**"); CVE-2014-4444 (OS X <10.10, "does not ensure that a Kerberos ticket is in the cache for the correct user, which allows local users to gain privileges… by leveraging a Fast User Switching login"); CVE-2004-0962 (ARD executes GUI app as root "when loginwindow is active via Fast User Switching").

**2005 → 2026, same subsystem, same class.** LoginWindow/SecurityAgent state at session-transition boundaries is the single most persistently defective isolation surface Apple ships.

---

## 4. Academic measurement — essentially nothing exists

**arXiv** (`https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?search_query=all:%22Shared+iPad%22`): **0 entries.** Also 0 for `"iOS parental control"` and for `macOS AND "privacy enforcement"`.

**Crossref** (`https://api.crossref.org/works?query=Shared+iPad+multi-user+security+isolation`): 8 results, **0 relevant** — top hits are iPad gesture HCI (2012), searchable encryption, steganographic file systems, MIMO relays.

**OpenAlex** — two sweeps completed before budget exhaustion; nothing on Shared iPad. Adjacent iOS-isolation work that *does* exist:
- **"OS-level Side Channels without Procfs: Exploring Cross-App Information Leakage on iOS"**, NDSS 2018, `https://doi.org/10.14722/ndss.2018.23260` — 33 citations. Cross-**app** leakage, not cross-user.
- **"Kobold: Evaluating Decentralized Access Control for Remote NSXPC Methods on iOS"**, IEEE S&P 2020, `https://doi.org/10.1109/sp40000.2020.00023` — 8 citations. Closest thing to a systematic measurement of an iOS access-control layer.
- "False Sense of Security: A Study on the Effectivity of Jailbreak Detection in Banking Apps", EuroS&P 2019, `https://doi.org/10.1109/eurosp.2019.00011` — 16 citations.
- "SoK: Understanding the Prevailing Security Vulnerabilities in TrustZone-assisted TEE Systems", IEEE S&P 2020, `https://doi.org/10.1109/sp40000.2020.00061` — 202 citations. ARM TrustZone, not SEP; adjacent only.

**FINDING (negative, clean): there is no published academic measurement of Shared iPad's isolation, of macOS fast-user-switching isolation, or of iOS parental-control circumvention.** Zero on arXiv, zero via Crossref, zero in the OpenAlex slices reached. The entire evidence base for §1 is Apple's own design documentation; the entire evidence base for §3 is Apple's own vulnerability disclosures. **No independent party has measured any of it.**

---

## Bottom line

1. **Shared iPad is a real per-user cryptographic boundary** — APFS volume + keybag + local keychain + uid + sandbox — but it is **gated behind organizational identity ownership** (Managed Apple Account + supervision + MDM), and its liveness **depends on iCloud**: eviction requires completed cloud sync, and without it new sign-ins fail. The machinery exists on consumer hardware; only the identity gate withholds it.

2. **iOS's consumer answer is not isolation, it is concealment.** Guided Access is single-app lockdown (endable with the child's own biometric, and it disables Emergency Services). Screen Time is explicitly disclaimed by Apple as "**not a new system security feature**." Locked/Hidden apps leak the app name through three documented side channels and yield to anyone holding the device passcode.

3. **The measured defects are all on macOS, all in session-transition state, and they recur.** "View the previous logged in user's desktop from the fast user switching screen" was fixed as three separate CVEs (2021-30908 → 2022-22656 → 2023-42935), each with the identical remediation string "improved state management." "View another user's screen" via screen sharing repeated too (2020-27893 → 2024-44248). Keychain local-attacker access is annual and unbroken 2021–2026, and now lands on iOS as well.

4. **Two silences worth reading as findings, not gaps.** Zero CVEs on Shared iPad, and zero CVEs on Guided Access. The second has a clear cause — Apple does not classify parental-control evasion as a vulnerability, so it never enters the pipeline, and a small market of third-party products now exists specifically to shore up Screen Time. The first has no such explanation, and with zero academic scrutiny alongside it, **the correct reading is unexamined, not sound.**

5. **TCC's grant model is container-bound, not principal-bound** — five separate third-party CVEs (GIMP, Insomnia, waveterm, Arduino IDE, and one more) exist purely because a bundled interpreter inherits its host bundle's TCC grants. Worth carrying into any capability design: a permission attached to a container is held by everything that container can execute.

**Unreached, do not fill from memory:** the Screen Time passcode weakness literature (Mysk's site is a JS SPA my fetch could not enumerate; the older Restrictions-passcode-hash-in-backup recovery went unverified), Apple's default offline sign-in grace-period value, and any statement on whether Secure Enclave material is partitioned per Shared iPad user — Apple's Shared iPad security page never mentions the Secure Enclave at all. OpenAlex budget was exhausted mid-sweep, so the academic negative rests on arXiv + Crossref plus two partial OpenAlex slices.
